<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kroměříž</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kremsier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kroměříž, in German Kremsier, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, long the summer seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, whose palace and gardens are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. From November 1848 to March 1849 the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> sat there and drafted a liberal constitution, but it was dissolved by the government before the draft could take effect.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kromeriz.jpg" alt="Kroměříž" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Frank from Wien (Vienna), AT · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krom%C4%9B%C5%99%C3%AD%C5%BE._Ausblick_vom_Schlossturm_(25007770188).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kroměříž lies on both banks of the Morava River in the Zlín Region, about 20 kilometres north-west of Zlín, where the Haná River flows into the Morava on the town's northern edge. Its name comes from the personal name Kroměžir, meaning Kroměžir's property. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a market village at the crossing of the Amber and Salt roads, at a ford across the Morava where a toll was collected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written mention, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name Cromezir, is in a document written between 1107 and 1125, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> bishop Jan II bought the settlement. In the thirteenth century it became the centre of a dominion of the Olomouc bishopric. Raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and Hungarians in 1241 and 1253 depopulated the settlement. Bishop Bruno von Schauenburg then rebuilt it, founding the market square on the hill above the old settlement, surrounding it with walls, building the Church of Saint Maurice and planting vineyards. The place was first called a town in 1266 and was officially promoted to a town in 1290.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were allowed to settle in the town in 1322, and in the mid-sixteenth century its Jewish community was the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> lost control of the town during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> in 1423 and regained it in 1455. Bishop Stanislav I Thurzo rebuilt the bishop's residence as a castle in late Gothic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style.</p><p>Swedish troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lennart-torstensson">Lennart Torstensson</a> plundered the town in 1643, burning most of its buildings, and a large fire struck in 1656. Bishop Karl II von Liechtenstein-Kastelkorn then rebuilt the town and had the castle completely rebuilt by 1686, founding a mint and ornamental gardens between 1666 and 1675. The Constitutive Imperial Congress sat in Kroměříž in 1848, and in August 1885 the Austrian and Russian emperors met there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kroměříž is a town of about 28,000 inhabitants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and is best known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> castle and gardens, added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1998. The historic centre is protected as an urban monument reservation. The Castle Gallery holds about 500 paintings, including a late work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a>, the Flaying of Marsyas. The Church of Saint Maurice retains its early Gothic appearance and holds the grave of Bishop Bruno von Schauenburg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kroměříž” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The revolutions of March 1848 drove Metternich into exile and forced the promise of constitutions, and in December 1848 Ferdinand abdicated in favour of his eighteen-year-old nephew Franz Joseph I. Prince Felix zu Schwarzenberg dissolved the <b>Kremsier</b> parliament in 1849, and the Sylvester Patent of 1851 restored absolutism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Austrian Reichstag of 1848–1849 sat at <b>Kroměříž</b>, where it drafted a federal constitution that was never put into force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
